# Git / GitHub — bypass local proxy (mandatory)

On this machine, **plain SSH to `github.com:22` often fails** (e.g. `Connection closed by 127.0.0.1 port 7897`, `kex_exchange_identification`) because traffic goes through a **local HTTP/SOCKS proxy**. **Do not** waste turns retrying or “exploring”; use the bypass below **every time** you run Git over SSH to GitHub.

## When this applies

Any **`git`** operation that uses **`git@github.com`** / SSH remote: **`push`**, **`fetch`**, **`pull`**, **`ls-remote`**, etc.

## Required workaround (pick one)

### A — One-shot command prefix (preferred for agents)

Use GitHub’s SSH **HTTPS port** so traffic does not hit the broken proxied path:

```bash
GIT_SSH_COMMAND='ssh -o Hostname=ssh.github.com -o Port=443 -o User=git' git <subcommand> ...
```

Example:

```bash
GIT_SSH_COMMAND='ssh -o Hostname=ssh.github.com -o Port=443 -o User=git' git push -u origin mvp-foundation
```

### B — Persistent user config (human machines)

In **`~/.ssh/config`**:

```ssh-config
Host github.com
  HostName ssh.github.com
  Port 443
  User git
```

Then normal `git push` works without `GIT_SSH_COMMAND`.

## Do not

- Rely on unsetting `HTTP_PROXY` alone for SSH — **SSH is not HTTP**; the failure mode here is specific to **GitHub SSH over port 22** vs **local proxy**.
- Change `origin` to HTTPS in the repo unless the user explicitly asks — prefer **A** or **B**.

## Evidence

GitHub documents SSH over port 443 (`ssh.github.com`). This matches the fix that succeeded for pushes in this project’s environment.
